    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d31">

                <pb facs="0028" n="24" sameAs="frus:frus1938v04/pg24"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">600.939/201: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-07-06T19:00:00+09:00">July 6, 1938—7
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 6—8:40 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>444. Tientsin’s 114, July 1, 6 p.m.;<anchor n="39" xml:id="d31-f1"/> and
                    Department’s 329 [<hi rendition="#italic">229</hi>], July 2, 7 p.m.,<anchor
                        n="40" xml:id="d31-f2"/> export embargo hides and skins. I presented this
                    case to the Minister for Foreign Affairs<anchor n="41" xml:id="d31-f3"/> during
                    the course of my recent interview with him<anchor n="42" xml:id="d31-f4"/> (our
                    437, July 4, 7 p.m.<anchor n="43" xml:id="d31-f5"/>) and today we delivered to
                    the Foreign Office an <hi rendition="#italic">aide-mémoire</hi> along the lines
                    desired by the Department.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Peiping for Tientsin and Hankow.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
